<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Tusculum</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 381 BCE (approx.) · capital Tusculum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latin hill town in the Alban Hills, once a leader of the Latins against Rome, which in 381 BCE became the first community given Roman citizenship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tusculum stood on a high ridge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> above modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frascati">Frascati</a>, overlooking the routes south-east from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Legend made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegonus">Telegonus</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a>, its founder. It was an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> town by the 6th century BCE, with links to Rome's last kings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its leading family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamilia-gens">Mamilii</a>, intermarried with the Tarquins; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/octavius-mamilius">Octavius Mamilius</a>, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>, is said to have led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Lake Regillus</a> around 496 BCE. Afterwards Tusculum became one of Rome's most loyal allies in the wars against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a>, and it hosted shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>, gods also linked to Regillus.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 381 BCE, after a brief suspicion of disloyalty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> granted Tusculum full citizenship while letting it keep its own magistrates, making it the first municipium. It produced leading Roman families, among them that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero's</a> villa there gave its name to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tusculan-disputations">Tusculan Disputations</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tusculum.jpg" alt="Tusculum" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Patafisik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tusculum_visto_dall%27alto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 381 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tusculum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Octavius Mamilius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tusculum (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-490&amp;focus=tusculum" aria-label="Tusculum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4989 689 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tusculum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5309 945L5309 947M5309 945L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5309 947h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-490&amp;focus=tusculum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
